WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.740
From audit findings to proven fixes.

2
00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:07.290
This is the video for module two of the professional SEO course.

3
00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:18.140
Amateurs hand a client a long list of everything imperfect. Professionals hand over a short, ordered plan where the first items clearly matter most.

4
00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:26.110
In this video you will triage the tools, score findings, write clear tickets, and check whether each fix worked.

5
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:36.900
Start with Search Console. URL Inspection shows whether a page is on Google, which canonical Google chose, and a live test of the page now.

6
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:53.150
The free Screaming Frog crawler checks up to 500 URLs on a laptop, and finds broken links, redirects, titles and canonicals. On small hosting, lower the crawl speed, and only crawl sites you have permission to audit.

7
00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:04.270
PageSpeed Insights shows field data from real users and a lab test. If field data is poor, real customers are suffering, so report that first.

8
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.620
Then check structured data, and add the site to Bing for a second view.

9
00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:23.800
Funmi audits an online bookshop in Ibadan. The Page indexing report shows 1,240 URLs not indexed. That number frightens clients, but many reasons are normal.

10
00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:30.380
For each reason she asks one question. Does this group contain pages the business needs in search?

11
00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:43.150
610 are old http addresses that redirect, and 380 are sorting pages with a proper canonical. Both are expected. No action.

12
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:53.850
Of 150 crawled but not indexed, 22 are real book pages with only a title and price. That is a finding: thin pages.

13
00:01:55.100 --> 00:02:01.800
Of 70 not found, 9 are still linked from the menu and blog. Finding: broken internal links.

14
00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:12.410
Of 30 noindex pages, 2 are the Nigerian authors and School textbooks categories. Those are hidden money pages, and the most urgent.

15
00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:15.820
So she reports three clear findings.

16
00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:26.320
Give each finding three scores from 1 to 5. Impact is how much it could change visibility or sales for pages the business cares about.

17
00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:31.690
Confidence is how sure you are, from evidence, that fixing it will help.

18
00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.100
Ease is how easy it is to implement.

19
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:52.770
Multiply them. For example, Ada found the homepage canonical pointing to an old staging domain. Five times five times five, 125. Her slow pages scored only 18, because they need a developer and more time.

20
00:02:54.020 --> 00:03:06.590
But some issues stop a site appearing at all. A sitewide noindex, a robots.txt that blocks everything, or canonicals to an old domain. These are critical. Report them the same day.

21
00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:16.200
A finding explains a problem. A ticket tells one person exactly what to do. It can live in a WhatsApp group or a shared sheet.

22
00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:30.140
Ada's ticket for 38 tour pages with the same title included all 38 new titles, written by her. Vague tickets like improve titles are the most common reason SEO advice never gets done.

23
00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:42.590
A recommendation is not a result. First, open the page source and confirm the change. If it does not show, ask the developer to clear the cache before you call it failed.

24
00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:47.300
Second, recrawl with the same settings and compare counts.

25
00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:55.100
Third, check Google's view with URL Inspection. Validation typically takes up to about two weeks.

26
00:03:56.350 --> 00:04:07.630
Chioma rewrote titles on 8 recipe pages of a food blog. She compared equal periods, 28 days before and 28 days after, and left out the week of the change.

27
00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:17.520
CTR on those pages rose from 1.80% to 2.70%, while position barely moved.

28
00:04:18.770 --> 00:04:28.800
She also kept 10 similar pages unchanged as a comparison group. Their CTR hardly moved, from 2.1% to 2.2%.

29
00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:36.320
The Status Dashboard showed no announced Google update. So she reports strong evidence, not proof.

30
00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:49.300
Say which number you mean. This is a rise of 0.9 percentage points, which is a relative rise of about 50%. Then record it in a shared change log, with the date.

31
00:04:50.550 --> 00:05:07.850
To remember. Triage by what matters to the business. Fix critical blockers first, then score the rest. Write tickets with clear acceptance criteria. Verify every fix three ways. And measure with equal periods, a comparison group and a dated change log.

32
00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:16.270
Now run a mini check on a site you can access, score your findings, write one full ticket, and start your change log.

33
00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:18.560
Continue with the lessons below.
